  keywords: ['Hercules family', 'Lyra', 'Cygnus', 'Aquila', 'Summer Triangle', 'Vega', 'Deneb', 'Altair', 'Hydra', 'Ophiuchus', 'Centaurus']
};

const faqData = [
  {
    question: 'What is the Hercules family of constellations?',
    answer: 'The Hercules family is the largest of the constellation families, containing 19 constellations associated with the hero Hercules and his labours. Its members include Hercules, Sagitta, Aquila, Lyra, Cygnus, Vulpecula, Hydra, Sextans, Crater, Corvus, Ophiuchus, Serpens, Scutum, Centaurus, Lupus, Corona Australis, Ara, Triangulum Australe, and Crux. They stretch across a huge swathe of the summer and southern skies.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the Summer Triangle?',
    answer: 'The Summer Triangle is a large, bright asterism formed by three first-magnitude stars from three different Hercules-family constellations: Vega in Lyra, Deneb in Cygnus, and Altair in Aquila. It is not an official constellation but one of the most useful signposts of the northern summer sky, riding high overhead on warm evenings and helping observers locate the constellations around it and the Milky Way running through its middle.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the brightest star in the Hercules family?',
    answer: 'Alpha Centauri, in Centaurus, is the brightest star in the family and the third-brightest in the entire night sky. It is also the closest star system to the Sun at 4.37 light-years, and includes Proxima Centauri, the nearest individual star of all. In the northern sky, Vega in Lyra is the family’s brightest star, ranking fifth overall and serving as a brilliant anchor of the Summer Triangle.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is the constellation Hydra so large?',
    answer: 'Hydra, the Water Snake, is the single largest of all 88 constellations, covering 1,303 square degrees — more than 3% of the entire sky. It stretches over a quarter of the way around the celestial sphere, from near Cancer in the north to Libra in the south. Despite its enormous length, Hydra is faint, with only one notably bright star, Alphard, the "solitary one," standing out in an otherwise empty stretch of sky.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Ophiuchus part of the zodiac?',
    answer: 'Ophiuchus, the Serpent Bearer, is a Hercules-family constellation that the Sun genuinely passes through for about 18 days each year, making it a de facto 13th zodiac constellation. However, it was never included in the traditional 12-sign zodiac, which was fixed in ancient times to match the 12 lunar months. So while Ophiuchus straddles the ecliptic, astrology has always left it out.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What deep-sky objects are in the Hercules family?',
    answer: 'The family is exceptionally rich. Hercules holds the Great Globular Cluster M13, one of the finest globulars in the northern sky. Lyra contains the famous Ring Nebula (M57). Cygnus, lying along the Milky Way, is packed with nebulae including the North America and Veil nebulae. Ophiuchus and Serpens border the vibrant star-forming regions near the galactic centre, and Centaurus hosts Omega Centauri, the largest and brightest globular cluster in the sky.'
  }
];
